<template>
    <heading>Appendix I: Literate Programming</heading>
    <explanation>
        <div class="epigraph">
            <div>
                Nothing about WEB is really new; I have simply combined a
                bunch of ideas that have been in the air for a long time.
            </div>
            <hr>
            <div>
                –Donald Knuth, p. 130, "Literate Programming"
            </div>
        </div>
        <div>
            <p>
                This appendix is for people familiar with Knuth's
                Literate Programming who want to know how it is
                related to lp5.
                lp5 is obviously related to Literate Programming,
                yet not the same.
                Some differences are structurally driven by the
                desire to reimagine the "web" of Literate Programming
                as the web that HTML5 natively supplies.
            </p>
            <p>
                When I say <q>Literate Programming</q> I am referring
                specifically to the concepts documented and implemented
                by Knuth.
                It is necessary to point this out because the
                generic-sounding nature of the name leads
                an amazing percentage of programmers who hear
                it to believe that it refers to nothing very specific at all.
                A survey of <q>Literate Programming</q> videos on YouTube
                reveals people who believe it refers to nearly any
                code which contains any documentation whatsoever!
            </p>
            <p>
                I believe if you polled all the programmers who have
                heard of Literate Programming,
                the overwhelming majority would not know,
                for example,
                that there is a directed graph at the heart of
                the techique, or what that is for.
                If Knuth jokingly took his <q>revenge</q> on
                <q>structured programming</q> by using similar
                language to name his new tools,
                language has had the last laugh,
                and helped prevent <q>Literate Programming</q>
                from being understood,
                let alone taken up,
                by a significant percentage of programmers.
            </p>
            <h3>What Does LP5 Take From Literate Programming?</h3>
            <p>
                Broadly speaking, there are two key concepts that lp5
                takes from Literate Programming.
                The first is the primacy of explanation,
                and the second is generalized decomposition.
            </p>
            <h4>The Primacy of Explanation.</h4>
            <p>
                The primacy of explanation in Literate Programming can
                be seen even in its basic structure.
                Perhaps the most "documentation" that the average
                modern programmer ever writes is in the form of
                comments embedded in the code.
                Tools are available to extract specifically structured
                comments from the code and create a separate document.
                Thus, the "documentation" is embedded in and subordinate to the
                code.
                In Literate Programming, the code is embedded in the documentation,
                or as I prefer to call it, explanation.
            </p>
            <p>
                Of course, these structures cannot <emph>enforce</emph>
                behavior.
                You are free to just plop your existing explanation-free
                code into a Literate Programming tool and refuse to
                explain it at all!
            </p>
            <p>
                Here lies one of the forces preventing the
                adoption of Literate Programming:
                a revulsion for writing.
                Alas, this revulsion also prevents ever experiencing
                the benefits of Literate Programming.
            </p>

            <p>
                took away from Literate Programming.
                First, lp5 takes the decomposition
                tools of Literate Programming.
                It is astonishing how often this is belittled or
                completely ignored in discussions of Literate Programming.
                Kibitzers will even claim that this was merely a
                crutch for ancient programming language restrictions
                on the ordering of code and has no use in
                modern languages.
            </p>
            <p>
                Literate Programming and lp5 allow nearly arbitrary degrees
                of ordering and decomposition of code.
                Programmers may <q>break a function down</q> into
                subfunctions merely because it becomes physically long
                rather than because it lacks cohesion or is not understandable.
                With all Literate Programming tools for any languages they target,
                this is completely unnecessary—one breaks functions
                down textually, and only breaks them down structurally
                when that actually makes sense for the structure of the code.
            </p>
            <p>
                Likewise, the always annoying problem of too many levels of indentation
                (or unreadably small indentation to make up for too many levels)
                pretty much disappears.
                Long before indentation levels become ungainly,
                one has generally textually decomposed the code into
                smaller sections.
            </p>
            <p>
                In 1984, long before any World Wide Web,
                Knuth was saying:
            </p><blockquote>
                [A]fter gaining experience with WEB,
                I have come to realize that there is no need to
                choose once and for all between top-down and
                bottom-up, because a program is best thought
                of as a web instead of as a tree.
            </blockquote>
            <p>
                Programming inventions like structured programming,
                object-oriented programming, etc., gave programmers
                no new computational power (it's Turing machines all the way down,
                after all), but provided new forms of expressing computation.
                These new forms of expression required experience to
                get used to and to evolve idioms that proved beneficial.
                The decompositional tools that Literate Programming
                and lp5 provide likewise make possible new forms of
                expressing computation.
                There's really not much point in talking about it
                until you've tried it and glimpsed the possibilities for
                new forms and styles.
            </p>
            <p>
                The second key concept lp5 takes from Literate Programming is,
                of course, the primacy of text explanations.
                Here again, I think most of writing about Literate Programming
                misses the mark.
                What all professional writers know that apparently
                most programmers don't know is this:
                writing is a form of thinking.
                Professional writers don't just write down
                what they're thinking
                (a whole lot more people would be writers if it was that simple),
                their writing is a part of their process of thinking.
                What this means for programmers is essentially this:
                <b>if you focus on explaining your code,
                    you will write different code.</b>
            </p>
            <p>
                The typical programmer seems to think writing explanations
                of code represents a failure to write clear code,
                or a mindless chore with little value,
                or a duplication of code that will need to be
                <q>maintained.</q>
                They don't understand that writing can be an intimate
                part of the process of thinking,
                giving you the ability to see things you would
                otherwise ignore.
            </p>
            <p>
                Quite apart from the specific techniques of Literate Programming,
                I can't count the number of times writing documentation
                for my code has altered the code.
                Often, this simply takes the form of realizing
                how overcomplicated things have become.
                Many times, as I read documentation for some
                API function that droned on with a seemingly endless
                list of cautions and special cases,
                I mightily wished the programmer who invented the API
                had tried to write an explanation for it first.
                They might have had the experience that they instead
                inflicted on all the users of their code:
                the experience of <q>My God, there has to be a simpler way!</q>
            </p>
            <h3>What Does LP5 Reject From Literate Programming?</h3>
            <p>
                Most importantly, lp5 rejects the core Knuth notion
                of programs as literature.
                I remember how excited I was when I
                read Knuth's paper <i>Literate Programming</i>
                in 1984.
                And how much less excited I was when I actually
                read his
                <i>T<span style="position:relative; top:.2em">E</span>X: The Program</i>.
                Where were the pictures?
                Where were the overviews, the architectural
                rationales, and so on?
                Much like reading a novel,
                if you wanted pictures and overviews and
                other non-linear slices of understanding,
                you were responsible for creating them yourself
                after reading the book.
            </p>
            <p>
                Do I want to read code <q>like a book?</q>
                No, I do not,
                no more than I want to traverse Harry Potter like
                a hyptertext pile of source code.
                Knuth said:
            </p><blockquote>
                My experiences have led me to believe that a person
                reading a program is, likewise, ready to comprehend it
                by learning its various parts in approximately the
                order in which it was written.
            </blockquote>
            But I do not want to learn the various parts of
            a good-sized program—I want to learn just
            the bits I need to know to do my work,
            be it finding/fixing a bug, adding a new feature,
            or whatever.
            And in the order that saves me the most time—hypertext
            order, in other words, jumping around to get
            to the information I need at that instant in time.
            <p></p>
            <p>
                However, do not reject all novels just because you
                did not care for one author's book.
                As Knuth says,
                <q>
                    I imagine that different programmers will converge
                    to quite different styles.
                </q>
                When Martin Ruckert published
                <a href="https://www.google.com/books/edition/Understanding_MP3/MN34-91z6qAC?hl=en&amp;gbpv=1&amp;pg=PT7&amp;printsec=frontcover">Understanding MP3</a>,
                a Literate Program that explains the ubiquitous
                audio coding format,
                there were wonderful pictures, expositions, rationales,
                etc.
                Ruckert explicitly wrote in a style designed to serve
                different needs,
                making it easy to dive into exactly the area of
                the topic you were interested in at the moment.
                Knuth's Literate Programming provides tools—what
                authors can produce with those tools is as varied
                as the authors themselves.
            </p>
            <p>
                This mostly boils down to differing goals.
                Knuth is concerned with creating finished, polished works,
                with the finality of committing ink to paper and paper
                to book publisher.
                I live a world where <q>finished</q> software is a mirage.
                Few programs are ever finished, they're always being maintained,
                extended, rethought.
            </p>
            <p>
                Of course, an unavoidable handicap of Knuth's
                consistent (relentless?) push to analogize with
                literature was that he was trying to create
                hypertext with ink and paper.
            </p>
            <p>
                blah
            </p>

        </div>
    </explanation>
</template>
